WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.905
Authority and scope come before collection, sharing,

00:00:02.905 --> 00:00:06.590
or action. A team should be able to answer basic questions before it begins:

00:00:06.590 --> 00:00:10.080
who requested the work, what decision it supports, what information is needed,

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:13.795
which systems or records are in scope, which jurisdictions or policies apply,

00:00:13.795 --> 00:00:17.120
who may receive the results, and who must review sensitive steps.

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:19.695
If the answers are not known, that is a coordination issue,

00:00:19.695 --> 00:00:21.200
not an invitation to improvise.

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:25.390
Jurisdiction is broader than geography. It can include national or regional law,

00:00:25.390 --> 00:00:29.230
employment rules, contractual limits, sector regulations, government requirements,

00:00:29.230 --> 00:00:33.960
data residency expectations, evidence rules, partner agreements, and internal policy.

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:35.490
A corporate fraud team, a SOC,

00:00:35.490 --> 00:00:39.140
and a public-sector coordination office may all use intelligence processes,

00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:41.780
but their authorities and constraints can differ sharply.

00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:45.340
Even inside one organization, separate subsidiaries, business units,

00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:47.690
or regulated functions may have different rules.

00:00:47.690 --> 00:00:51.770
This course does not provide legal advice or jurisdiction-specific procedures. The

00:00:51.770 --> 00:00:55.850
operational lesson is to recognize boundary conditions and route uncertainty to qualified

00:00:55.850 --> 00:00:56.360
reviewers.

00:00:56.360 --> 00:00:59.630
Legal, privacy, compliance, records, HR, policy owners,

00:00:59.630 --> 00:01:02.700
and governance stakeholders are not obstacles to coordination.

00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:06.240
They are part of lawful coordination. They help determine what information may be

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:06.770
collected,

00:01:06.770 --> 00:01:09.535
how it can be retained, which identifiers should be minimized,

00:01:09.535 --> 00:01:11.310
whether cross-border sharing is allowed,

00:01:11.310 --> 00:01:13.790
and whether an action requires additional approval.

00:01:13.790 --> 00:01:18.360
Scope also protects the quality of analysis. A defined scope keeps the team focused on the

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:20.880
decision rather than chasing every interesting lead.

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:24.925
It limits unnecessary exposure of personal data, sensitive business information,

00:01:24.925 --> 00:01:27.480
partner data, or restricted source material.

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:31.295
It makes escalation clearer because everyone understands what the effort is intended to

00:01:31.295 --> 00:01:31.625
answer.

00:01:31.625 --> 00:01:35.688
When authority is unclear, pause and document the uncertainty. Ask for review.

00:01:35.688 --> 00:01:37.880
Record who approved the scope, what limits apply,

00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:40.100
and what conditions would require reapproval.

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:45.070
That discipline protects the organization, the analyst, the affected people or entities,

00:01:45.070 --> 00:01:46.720
and the usefulness of the intelligence product.
